rom its pairs: (3,1) => b=1; (2,0) => b=0; (1,3) => b=3; (0,1) => b=1; (0,2) => b=2. So b can be 0,1,2,3 (all four). So b can be any domain value.\n\nc2 gives possible b values: from its pairs: (1,3) => b=1; (2,2) => b=2; (2,3) => b=2; (0,2) => b=0; (0,0) => b=0. So b can be 0,1,2.\n\nThus intersection: b ∈ {0,1,2}. So b cannot be 3.\n\nThus b ∈ {0,1,2}.\n\nNow we also have c9: (b,e) ∈ {(1,2), (3,1)}. So b must be 1 or 3. Intersection with b ∈ {0,1,2} yields b=1 only. So b must be 1. So indeed b=1 is forced.\n\nThus b=1.\n\nNow c2 with b=1: only c=3 (pair (1,3)). So c=3.\n\nThus c=3.\n\nNow c3 with c=3: allowed d values: (3,2) and (3,1). So d ∈ {2,1}.\n\nNow c4 with each d:\n\n- If d=2: allowed a values: (2,3) only? Actually c4 includes (2,3) meaning (d=2,a=3). So a=3.\n\n- If d=1: allowed a values: (1,2) and (1,0). So a ∈ {2,0}.\n\nNow c1 must be satisfied: (a,b) must be allowed.\n\n- For d=2, a=3, b=1: (3,1) is allowed (c1 includes (3,1)). So valid.\n\n- For d=1, a=2, b=1: (2,1) is not in c1. So invalid.\n\n- For d=1, a=0, b=1: (0,1) is allowed (c1 includes (0,1)). So valid.\n\nThus indeed only two left assignments: (3,1,3,2) and (0,1,3,1). So our enumeration is correct.\n\nNow we need to check cross constraints for each.\n\nc9: b=1 → e=2. So e=2 for both.\n\nc10: d=2 → g=1; d=1 → g=0.\n\nNow c5: (e,f) allowed: e=2 → only f=3.\n\nThus f=3 for both.\n\nc6: (f,g) allowed: f=3 → g must be 1 or 2.\n\nThus for case d=2 (g=1) it's allowed. For case d=1 (g=0) it's not allowed. So case d=1 invalid.\n\nThus only left assignment (3,1,3,2) works.\n\nThus indeed only one left tuple.\n\nThus we need to output JSON with that tuple.\n\nNow we must ensure the envelope matches the template exactly except for the tuple. The template includes fields: \"intent\", \"message_id\", \"payload\", \"problem_id\", \"read_refs\", \"recipients\", \"reply_to\", \"schema_version\", \"sender\". The payload.claim has fields: \"claim_id\", \"dependencies\", \"provenance\", \"revision\", \"state_revision\", \"status\", \"value\". The value includes \"component\", \"tuples\", \"variables\". So we need to replace the placeholder tuple.\n\nThus final JSON is as above.\n\nNow check any formatting constraints: The problem says \"Return exactly one JSON protocol envelope matching the supplied template; no prose outside JSON. Do not wrap in markdown code blocks or backticks. Output raw unformatted JSON starting with { and ending with }.\"\n\nThus we must output raw JSON without any extra whitespace? It doesn't forbid whitespace; just raw unformatted JSON. Usually whitespace is allowed but it's okay. However, to be safe, we can output with normal indentation? The template includes indentation. The instruction says \"no prose outside JSON\". It doesn't forbid pretty printing. So we can output with newlines and indentation as in template.\n\nThus we should output exactly the JSON with the updated tuple.\n\nDouble-check that all fields are correct:\n\n- \"intent\": \"PROPOSE\"\n- \"message_id\": \"csp-5103-left-proposer-1\"\n- \"payload\": {...}\n- \"problem_id\": \"csp-5103\"\n- \"read_refs\": array with two objects: first referencing problem:csp-5103 revision 1 state_revision 1; second referencing claim:left-tuples revision 0 state_revision 0.\n- \"recipients\": [\"right-reviewer\",\"shared-integrator\"]\n- \"reply_to\": null\n- \"schema_version\": 1\n- \"sender\": \"left-proposer\"\n\nAll good.\n\nOne nuance: The claim's \"dependencies\" includes an object with \"claim_revision\": 1, \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-5103\". The template shows that. So we keep that.\n\nNow check that we haven't inadvertently changed any other fields. We only changed the \"tuples\" inside value.\n\nThus final answer is the JSON envelope.\n\nWe should output exactly that.\n\nThus the answer is:\n\n{\n  \"intent\": \"PROPOSE\",\n  \"message_id\": \"csp-5103-left-proposer-1\",\n  \"payload\": {\n    \"claim\": {\n      \"claim_id\": \"left-tuples\",\n      \"dependencies\": [\n        {\n          \"claim_revision\": 1,\n          \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-5103\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"provenance\": {\n        \"message_id\": \"csp-5103-left-proposer-1\"\n      },\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1,\n      \"status\": \"active\",\n      \"value\": {\n        \"component\": \"left\",\n        \"tuples\": [\n          [\n            3,\n            1,\n            3,\n            2\n          ]\n        ],\n        \"variables\": [\n          \"a\",\n          \"b\",\n          \"c\",\n          \"d\"\n        ]\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"problem_id\": \"csp-5103\",\n  \"read_refs\": [\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-5103\",\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1\n    },\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"claim:left-tuples\",\n      \"revision\": 0,\n      \"state_revision\": 0\n    }\n  ],\n  \"recipients\": [\n    \"right-reviewer\",\n    \"shared-integrator\"\n  ],\n  \"reply_to\": null,\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"sender\": \"left-proposer\"\n}\n\nWe should ensure that there is no trailing comma after the last element in any array. The above appears correct.\n\nThus output that.","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The user asks: \"Solve the finite constraint task from the complete immutable public packet below. Return exactly one JSON protocol envelope matching the supplied template; no prose outside JSON. Do not wrap in markdown code blocks or backticks. Output raw unformatted JSON starting with { and ending with }.\"\n\nWe have a CSP problem defined: variables a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h each domain {0,1,2,3}. Ten constraints (c1..c10) each specify allowed pairs for variable pairs. The left component includes variables a,b,c,d. We need to \"Enumerate ALL and ONLY satisfying assignments for component left, sorted lexicographically. Edit only payload.claim.value.tuples in the envelope. Each row contains exactly four domain integers in the listed variable order.\"\n\nThus we need to find all assignments (a,b,c,d) that satisfy all constraints that involve those variables (i.e., constraints c1..c4 directly involve left variables, plus constraints c9 (b,e) and c10 (d,g) involve cross variables, and also constraints c5..c8 involve right variables e,f,g,h and cross constraints c9,c10 involve left variables with right variables. So to satisfy all constraints, the left assignments must be such that there exist assignments for right variables e,f,g,h (domain 0-3) that satisfy all constraints (including cross constraints). So we need to find all possible left assignments that can be extended to a full solution (a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h) that satisfies all constraints.\n\nThus we need to enumerate all satisfying assignments for left component, i.e., all tuples (a,b,c,d) for which there exists at least one full solution. The problem wants \"Enumerate ALL and ONLY satisfying assignments for component left, sorted lexicographically.\" So we need to compute the projection of the solution space onto left variables.\n\nThus we need to solve the CSP. Let's denote variables and constraints:\n\nDomain D = {0,1,2,3}.\n\nConstraints:\n\nc1: (a,b) allowed pairs: [3,1], [2,0], [1,3], [0,1], [0,2].\nc2: (b,c) allowed pairs: [1,3], [2,2], [2,3], [0,2], [0,0].\nc3: (c,d) allowed pairs: [3,2], [1,3], [3,1], [2,0], [2,1].\nc4: (d,a) allowed pairs: [2,3], [1,2], [3,0], [1,0], [0,3].\nc5: (e,f) allowed pairs: [2,3], [0,2], [1,2], [1,1], [0,1].\nc6: (f,g) allowed pairs: [3,1], [2,1], [1,0], [3,2], [0,2].\nc7: (g,h) allowed pairs: [1,0], [2,3], [3,0], [2,2], [2,0].\nc8: (h,e) allowed pairs: [0,2], [0,3], [3,0], [1,1], [0,0].\nc9: (b,e) allowed pairs: [1,2], [3,1].\nc10: (d,g) allowed pairs: [2,1], [1,0].\n\nWe need all assignments for a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h that satisfy all constraints. Then we collect all distinct (a,b,c,d) from those solutions, sorted lexicographically (presumably standard order: compare a first, then b, then c, then d). Then output JSON envelope with that list of tuples.\n\nWe need to compute the projection.\n\nWe can try to solve manually or using systematic reasoning. Since domain size is 4, total combos for left variables is 4^4 = 256. Could brute force manually with reasoning, but better to systematically enumerate using logic.\n\nWe need to incorporate constraints linking left and right variables: c9 (b,e) and c10 (d,g). So for each left assignment, we need to see if we can assign e,f,g,h to satisfy constraints c5-c8 and cross constraints.\n\nThus we need to find all left assignments that have at least one extension.\n\nApproach: We can treat constraints as a graph. Let's denote allowed pairs for each constraint.\n\nList constraints in terms of allowed pairs:\n\nc1: (a,b) ∈ {(3,1), (2,0), (1,3), (0,1), (0,2)}.\n\nc2: (b,c) ∈ {(1,3), (2,2), (2,3), (0,2), (0,0)}.\n\nc3: (c,d) ∈ {(3,2), (1,3), (3,1), (2,0), (2,1)}.\n\nc4: (d,a) ∈ {(2,3), (1,2), (3,0), (1,0), (0,3)}.\n\nc5: (e,f) ∈ {(2,3), (0,2), (1,2), (1,1), (0,1)}.\n\nc6: (f,g) ∈ {(3,1), (2,1), (1,0), (3,2), (0,2)}.\n\nc7: (g,h) ∈ {(1,0), (2,3), (3,0), (2,2), (2,0)}.\n\nc8: (h,e) ∈ {(0,2), (0,3), (3,0), (1,1), (0,0)}.\n\nc9: (b,e) ∈ {(1,2), (3,1)}.\n\nc10: (d,g) ∈ {(2,1), (1,0)}.\n\nWe need to find all combos for a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h satisfying all these.\n\nWe can try to solve using constraint propagation manually. However, it's a moderately sized CSP; we can attempt to enumerate left possibilities systematically and then check if there exists any right assignment.\n\nGiven that domain is small, we could try to generate all possible left assignments (256) and then for each, see if there is any possible (e,f,g,h) satisfying constraints c5-c8 and cross constraints c9 and c10 given a,b,c,d.\n\nWe can try to reason about constraints c9 and c10: they restrict e based on b and g based on d.\n\nSpecifically:\n\nc9: (b,e) ∈ {(1,2), (3,1)}. So for a given b, e must be a specific value (or maybe none). Let's list possible (b,e) pairs:\n\n- If b=1, then e must be 2 (since (1,2) allowed).\n- If b=3, then e must be 1 (since (3,1) allowed).\n- If b=0, no allowed e (since no pair with b=0).\n- If b=2, no allowed e.\n\nThus b must be either 1 or 3 for any solution; otherwise left assignment invalid.\n\nc10: (d,g) ∈ {(2,1), (1,0)}. So for a given d:\n\n- If d=2, then g must be 1.\n- If d=1, then g must be 0.\n- If d=0, no allowed g.\n- If d=3, no allowed g.\n\nThus d must be 1 or 2.\n\nThus left variables must satisfy b∈{1,3} and d∈{1,2}. Additionally they must satisfy constraints c1-c4.\n\nThus we need to find all (a,b,c,d) where b∈{1,3}, d∈{1,2}, and (a,b)∈c1, (b,c)∈c2, (c,d)∈c3, (d,a)∈c4.\n\nThen for each such left assignment, we need to see if there exist e,f,g,h satisfying c5-c8 and cross constraints c9 (already determines e from b) and c10 (determines g from d). Then we need to check if we can assign f and h consistent with other constraints.\n\nThus we reduce problem to checking existence of f and h given e and g determined.\n\nSpecifically:\n\nGiven b, e is forced: if b=1 → e=2; if b=3 → e=1.\n\nGiven d, g is forced: if d=1 → g=0; if d=2 → g=1.\n\nNow we need to find f and h such that:\n\nc5: (e,f) allowed: e,f ∈ allowed pairs.\n\nc6: (f,g) allowed: f,g ∈ allowed pairs.\n\nc7: (g,h) allowed: g,h ∈ allowed pairs.\n\nc8: (h,e) allowed: h,e ∈ allowed pairs.\n\nThus we need to find f and h values (0-3) satisfying these constraints given e and g.\n\nThus for each left assignment (a,b,c,d) we can compute e and g deterministically, then we need to check if there exists f and h such that:\n\n- (e,f) ∈ C5 (allowed pairs)\n- (f,g) ∈ C6\n- (g,h) ∈ C7\n- (h,e) ∈ C8\n\nThus we need to check if there is a path through these constraints: e → f → g → h → e.\n\nThus we need to find f and h values such that these constraints hold.\n\nThus we can treat as a small CSP of two variables f and h with constraints linking to e,g.\n\nThus for each left assignment we can test existence of f and h.\n\nThus the problem reduces to enumerating left assignments that satisfy c1-c4 and have b∈{1,3}, d∈{1,2} (but those are implied by c1-c4 maybe). Then for each, compute e,g and check if there is f,h solution.\n\nThus we need to enumerate all left assignments (a,b,c,d) meeting c1-c4, then filter those where b=1 or 3, d=1 or 2, and then test existence of f,h.\n\nThus we need to enumerate all possible (a,b,c,d) that satisfy constraints c1-c4.\n\nWe can enumerate all combos for a,b,c,d meeting c1-c4, then filter.\n\nLet's list c1 allowed pairs for (a,b). We'll need to consider all possible a and b values.\n\nc1 allowed pairs:\n\n(3,1)\n(2,0)\n(1,3)\n(0,1)\n(0,2)\n\nThus possible (a,b) combos: (3,1), (2,0), (1,3), (0,1), (0,2).\n\nThus a can be 0,1,2,3; b can be 0,1,2,3. So limited combos.\n\nc2 allowed pairs for (b,c): (1,3), (2,2), (2,3), (0,2), (0,0).\n\nThus for each b, possible c values:\n\n- If b=0: c ∈ {2,0} (pairs (0,2) and (0,0)).\n- If b=1: c = 3 (pair (1,3)).\n- If b=2: c ∈ {2,3} (pairs (2,2) and (2,3)).\n- If b=3: no allowed c (since no pair with b=3). So b cannot be 3 for any solution? Wait c2 includes (1,3) but not (3,*). So b=3 is not allowed. However c9 restricts b to be 1 or 3. So b=3 is not allowed by c2. So b must be 1 (since b=3 not allowed by c2). So b must be 1. Good.\n\nThus b=1 is forced. Then e from c9: if b=1 → e=2. So e=2.\n\nThus b=1 is the only viable value.\n\nThus we have b=1.\n\nNow c2 with b=1: allowed c = 3 (only). So c=3.\n\nThus c=3.\n\nNow c3 allowed pairs for (c,d): (3,2), (1,3), (3,1), (2,0), (2,1).\n\nGiven c=3, possible d values from c3: look at pairs where first component is 3: (3,2) and (3,1). So d ∈ {2,1}.\n\nThus d can be 2 or 1.\n\nNow c4 allowed pairs for (d,a): (2,3), (1,2), (3,0), (1,0), (0,3).\n\nGiven d possibilities:\n\n- If d=2: allowed a values from (2,3) only? Actually c4 includes (2,3) meaning (d=2, a=3). So a=3.\n\n- If d=1: allowed a values: (1,2) and (1,0). So a ∈ {2,0}.\n\nThus possible (a,b,c,d) combos:\n\nCase 1: d=2, a=3, b=1, c=3.\n\nThus (a,b,c,d) = (3,1,3,2).\n\nCase 2: d=1, a=2, b=1, c=3.\n\nThus (2,1,3,1).\n\nCase 3: d=1, a=0, b=1, c=3.\n\nThus (0,1,3,1).\n\nThus these three combos satisfy c1-c4.\n\nBut we also need to check that these combos satisfy c1: (a,b) allowed. Let's verify:\n\nCase 1: (a,b) = (3,1). c1 includes (3,1) - yes.\n\nCase 2: (a,b) = (2,1). Is (2,1) in c1? c1 allowed pairs: (3,1), (2,0), (1,3), (0,1), (0,2). (2,1) is not listed. So case 2 is invalid because (a,b) must be one of allowed pairs. So case 2 fails c1.\n\nCase 3: (a,b) = (0,1). c1 includes (0,1) - yes.\n\nThus only case 1 and case 3 are valid.\n\nThus left assignments possible: (a,b,c,d) = (3,1,3,2) and (0,1,3,1). But we also need to check b∈{1,3} (true) and d∈{1,2} (true). So both are possible.\n\nThus we have two possible left assignments that satisfy constraints c1-c4.\n\nNow we need to check if there exists f and h for each left assignment.\n\nWe need to compute e and g for each left assignment.\n\nRecall:\n\n- b=1 → e=2 (from c9). So e=2 for both.\n\n- d determines g: if d=2 → g=1; if d=1 → g=0.\n\nThus:\n\nCase 1: (a,b,c,d) = (3,1,3,2). Then d=2 → g=1.\n\nCase 3: (a,b,c,d) = (0,1,3,1). Then d=1 → g=0.\n\nThus we have:\n\nCase 1: e=2, g=1.\n\nCase 2: e=2, g=0.\n\nNow we need to find f and h such that:\n\nc5: (e,f) allowed: e=2, so allowed f values from c5 pairs where first component is 2.\n\nc5 allowed pairs: (2,3), (0,2), (1,2), (1,1), (0,1). So only pair with first component 2 is (2,3). So f must be 3.\n\nThus for both cases, e=2 forces f=3.\n\nThus f=3.\n\nNow c6: (f,g) allowed: f=3, g depends.\n\nc6 allowed pairs: (3,1), (2,1), (1,0), (3,2), (0,2). So for f=3, allowed g values are 1 (pair (3,1)) or 2 (pair (3,2)). So g must be either 1 or 2.\n\nThus g must be 1 or 2.\n\nNow for each case:\n\n- Case 1: g=1. That matches allowed (3,1) => OK.\n\n- Case 2: g=0. But g=0 is not allowed with f=3 (since (3,0) not in c6). So case 2 fails because g=0 is not allowed.\n\nThus only case 1 passes c6.\n\nThus left assignment (0,1,3,1) is invalid because g=0 not allowed.\n\nThus only left assignment (3,1,3,2) yields a consistent f,g.\n\nNow we need to check c7: (g,h) allowed: g=1, allowed h values from c7.\n\nc7 allowed pairs: (1,0), (2,3), (3,0), (2,2), (2,0). So for g=1, the only pair is (1,0). So h must be 0.\n\nThus h=0.\n\nNow c8: (h,e) allowed: h=0, e=2. Check c8 allowed pairs: (0,2), (0,3), (3,0), (1,1), (0,0). So (0,2) is allowed. So c8 satisfied.\n\nThus we have a consistent full assignment:\n\na=3, b=1, c=3, d=2, e=2, f=3, g=1, h=0.\n\nThus left component tuple (a,b,c,d) = (3,1,3,2) is a satisfying assignment.\n\nThus the projection onto left variables yields exactly one tuple: [3,1,3,2].\n\nThus the answer: we need to output JSON envelope with payload.claim.value.tuples containing that tuple (the list of tuples). The envelope template is given. We need to edit only payload.claim.value.tuples in the envelope. The template includes a placeholder tuple [[0,0,0,0]].\n\nThus we need to replace that with [[3,1,3,2]].\n\nAlso need to ensure all other fields remain unchanged, except maybe we need to adjust any fields that refer to claim id? The template includes claim_id \"left-tuples\". It also includes dependencies referencing problem:csp-5103 revision 1. That's fine.\n\nWe need to output exactly one JSON envelope matching the supplied template, with the updated tuples.\n\nThus we need to produce:\n\n{\n  \"intent\": \"PROPOSE\",\n  \"message_id\": \"csp-5103-left-proposer-1\",\n  \"payload\": {\n    \"claim\": {\n      \"claim_id\": \"left-tuples\",\n      \"dependencies\": [\n        {\n          \"claim_revision\": 1,\n          \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-5103\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"provenance\": {\n        \"message_id\": \"csp-5103-left-proposer-1\"\n      },\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1,\n      \"status\": \"active\",\n      \"value\": {\n        \"component\": \"left\",\n        \"tuples\": [\n          [\n            3,\n            1,\n            3,\n            2\n          ]\n        ],\n        \"variables\": [\n          \"a\",\n          \"b\",\n          \"c\",\n          \"d\"\n        ]\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"problem_id\": \"csp-5103\",\n  \"read_refs\": [\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-5103\",\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1\n    },\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"claim:left-tuples\",\n      \"revision\": 0,\n      \"state_revision\": 0\n    }\n  ],\n  \"recipients\": [\n    \"right-reviewer\",\n    \"shared-integrator\"\n  ],\n  \"reply_to\": null,\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"sender\": \"left-proposer\"\n}\n\nMake sure JSON formatting is correct: no trailing commas, proper quoting. The numbers should be integers, not strings.\n\nThus we need to output exactly this JSON.\n\nDouble-check that we haven't missed any other left assignments.\n\nWe earlier found only two left assignments that satisfy c1-c4: (3,1,3,2) and (0,1,3,1). The second fails later due to f,g constraints. So indeed only one left tuple.\n\nWe should double-check if there could be any other left assignments that satisfy c1-c4 but we missed due to b being forced to 1. Let's re-evaluate constraints thoroughly.\n\nConstraints:\n\nc1: (a,b) ∈ {(3,1), (2,0), (1,3), (0,1), (0,2)}.\n\nc2: (b,c) ∈ {(1,3), (2,2), (2,3), (0,2), (0,0)}.\n\nc3: (c,d) ∈ {(3,2), (1,3), (3,1), (2,0), (2,1)}.\n\nc4: (d,a) ∈ {(2,3), (1,2), (3,0), (1,0), (0,3)}.\n\nThus we need to find all combos (a,b,c,d) satisfying these constraints.\n\nWe can treat as a path a->b->c->d->a.\n\nWe can do a systematic enumeration.\n\nApproach: Enumerate all possible b values allowed by c1 and c2.\n\nc1 gives possible b values: from its pairs: (3,1) => b=1; (2,0) => b=0; (1,3) => b=3; (0,1) => b=1; (0,2) => b=2. So b can be 0,1,2,3 (all four). So b can be any domain value.\n\nc2 gives possible b values: from its pairs: (1,3) => b=1; (2,2) => b=2; (2,3) => b=2; (0,2) => b=0; (0,0) => b=0. So b can be 0,1,2.\n\nThus intersection: b ∈ {0,1,2}. So b cannot be 3.\n\nThus b ∈ {0,1,2}.\n\nNow we also have c9: (b,e) ∈ {(1,2), (3,1)}. So b must be 1 or 3. Intersection with b ∈ {0,1,2} yields b=1 only. So b must be 1. So indeed b=1 is forced.\n\nThus b=1.\n\nNow c2 with b=1: only c=3 (pair (1,3)). So c=3.\n\nThus c=3.\n\nNow c3 with c=3: allowed d values: (3,2) and (3,1). So d ∈ {2,1}.\n\nNow c4 with each d:\n\n- If d=2: allowed a values: (2,3) only? Actually c4 includes (2,3) meaning (d=2,a=3). So a=3.\n\n- If d=1: allowed a values: (1,2) and (1,0). So a ∈ {2,0}.\n\nNow c1 must be satisfied: (a,b) must be allowed.\n\n- For d=2, a=3, b=1: (3,1) is allowed (c1 includes (3,1)). So valid.\n\n- For d=1, a=2, b=1: (2,1) is not in c1. So invalid.\n\n- For d=1, a=0, b=1: (0,1) is allowed (c1 includes (0,1)). So valid.\n\nThus indeed only two left assignments: (3,1,3,2) and (0,1,3,1). So our enumeration is correct.\n\nNow we need to check cross constraints for each.\n\nc9: b=1 → e=2. So e=2 for both.\n\nc10: d=2 → g=1; d=1 → g=0.\n\nNow c5: (e,f) allowed: e=2 → only f=3.\n\nThus f=3 for both.\n\nc6: (f,g) allowed: f=3 → g must be 1 or 2.\n\nThus for case d=2 (g=1) it's allowed. For case d=1 (g=0) it's not allowed. So case d=1 invalid.\n\nThus only left assignment (3,1,3,2) works.\n\nThus indeed only one left tuple.\n\nThus we need to output JSON with that tuple.\n\nNow we must ensure the envelope matches the template exactly except for the tuple. The template includes fields: \"intent\", \"message_id\", \"payload\", \"problem_id\", \"read_refs\", \"recipients\", \"reply_to\", \"schema_version\", \"sender\". The payload.claim has fields: \"claim_id\", \"dependencies\", \"provenance\", \"revision\", \"state_revision\", \"status\", \"value\". The value includes \"component\", \"tuples\", \"variables\". So we need to replace the placeholder tuple.\n\nThus final JSON is as above.\n\nNow check any formatting constraints: The problem says \"Return exactly one JSON protocol envelope matching the supplied template; no prose outside JSON. Do not wrap in markdown code blocks or backticks. Output raw unformatted JSON starting with { and ending with }.\"\n\nThus we must output raw JSON without any extra whitespace? It doesn't forbid whitespace; just raw unformatted JSON. Usually whitespace is allowed but it's okay. However, to be safe, we can output with normal indentation? The template includes indentation. The instruction says \"no prose outside JSON\". It doesn't forbid pretty printing. So we can output with newlines and indentation as in template.\n\nThus we should output exactly the JSON with the updated tuple.\n\nDouble-check that all fields are correct:\n\n- \"intent\": \"PROPOSE\"\n- \"message_id\": \"csp-5103-left-proposer-1\"\n- \"payload\": {...}\n- \"problem_id\": \"csp-5103\"\n- \"read_refs\": array with two objects: first referencing problem:csp-5103 revision 1 state_revision 1; second referencing claim:left-tuples revision 0 state_revision 0.\n- \"recipients\": [\"right-reviewer\",\"shared-integrator\"]\n- \"reply_to\": null\n- \"schema_version\": 1\n- \"sender\": \"left-proposer\"\n\nAll good.\n\nOne nuance: The claim's \"dependencies\" includes an object with \"claim_revision\": 1, \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-5103\". The template shows that. So we keep that.\n\nNow check that we haven't inadvertently changed any other fields. We only changed the \"tuples\" inside value.\n\nThus final answer is the JSON envelope.\n\nWe should output exactly that.\n\nThus the answer is:\n\n{\n  \"intent\": \"PROPOSE\",\n  \"message_id\": \"csp-5103-left-proposer-1\",\n  \"payload\": {\n    \"claim\": {\n      \"claim_id\": \"left-tuples\",\n      \"dependencies\": [\n        {\n          \"claim_revision\": 1,\n          \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-5103\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"provenance\": {\n        \"message_id\": \"csp-5103-left-proposer-1\"\n      },\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1,\n      \"status\": \"active\",\n      \"value\": {\n        \"component\": \"left\",\n        \"tuples\": [\n          